<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulugh Muhammad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1405">1405</a>–<a href="/years/1445">1445</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ulugh Muhammad was a Chinggisid khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> who, after losing power in the struggles of the early 15th century, moved to the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and around 1438 founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan</a>. He defeated and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-ii-of-moscow">Vasily II of Moscow</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> in 1445, the year he was killed by his son Mahmud. Another son, Qasim, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>'s service and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasimov">Kasimov</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulugh-muhammad.jpg" alt="Ulugh Muhammad" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Kirillin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahmud_reconstruction_(2023-03-07)_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ulugh Muhammad was a descendant of Jochi and so of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, most likely a son of Jalal ad-Din and grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>, though he may have descended from Hassan Jefai, a relative of Tokhtamysh. His nickname Ulugh, meaning older or large, set him apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuchuk-muhammad">Küchük Muhammad</a>, the younger or small. Orientalists wrote his name as Ulanus. He first came to power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> after the death of Yeremferden.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His main rival was his cousin Dawlat Berdi, son of Yeremferden. Holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> for much of his reign, Ulugh Muhammad was seen as the most legitimate khan, though his rival took the city after the Siege of Sarai in 1420 and kept it for two years. Driven out by Baraq Khan, he fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, won the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas the Great</a> and recaptured Sarai. He extended the Horde's power to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, made friends with the Ottoman Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> and sent an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1428–1429.</p><p>In 1431 he judged the succession in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in favour of Vasili II, grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a>. After Vytautas died he backed Sigismund Kęstutaitis against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svitrigaila">Švitrigaila</a> for the Lithuanian throne, while Švitrigaila and Vasili II supported his rivals. He lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> in 1436 and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, then quarrelled with the Crimeans and led 3,000 men north to take the border town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belyov">Belyov</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1437 he defeated a large army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-shemyaka">Dmitry Shemyaka</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belyov">Belyov</a>. Moving to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> in 1438 and separated it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. In 1439 he raided Muscovy, burning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a> and the outskirts of Moscow. Nothing is known of him from 1439 to 1444. In 1444–45 he occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a> and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murom">Murom</a>. His son Mustafa died fighting near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> in 1444, and his son Qasim Khan, sent to collect the ransom of Vasili II after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>, founded the Qasim Khanate in 1452.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ulugh Muhammad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Yury of Zvenigorod and his cousins Vasily Kosoy and Dmitry Shemyaka; Vasily was captured by the Tatars of <b>Ulugh Muhammad</b> in 1445 and blinded by his rivals in 1446, yet regained the throne with the backing of the church and the boyars, and his victory ended claims by…</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>One view dates it to 1437 or 1438, when <b>Ulugh Muhammad</b>, a former khan of the Golden Horde, took power in Kazan with the support of the local nobility.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1452 Vasily II of Moscow granted the town of Gorodets Meshchersky on the Oka, later renamed Kasimov, to Qasim, a son of <b>Ulugh Muhammad</b>, founder of the Khanate of Kazan, who had entered Moscow's service.</blockquote><a href="/state/qasim-khanate">Qasim Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.79,49.11&amp;year=1431" aria-label="Kazan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5620 262 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ulugh Muhammad"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 532h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 532h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=55.79,49.11&amp;year=1431">What was here in 1431? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
